{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310856,"nonce":"iXL8HhfqS1Uq0WQ3db/2iP626sg=","title":"Beadie ships a complete OSR subsystem that rayzor has never wired; the whole-module LLVM compile is the workaround for its absence","message":"The `beadie` dependency (darmie/beadie) ships a COMPLETE OSR subsystem that\nrayzor does not use at all.\n\nBeadie side (`crates/beadie-core/src/osr.rs`, `bead.rs`, `beadie-backend/src/lib.rs`):\n- `OsrEntry { site: u64, code: *mut () }`, `OsrTable { generation, entries }`\n- `Bead::osr_entry(site) -\u003e Option\u003c*mut ()\u003e` — lock-free probe: state-gated,\n  epoch-pinned, generation-checked, binary search. Safe on the back-edge path.\n- `BackendAdapter::on_invoke_osr(bound, factory) -\u003e Option\u003c*mut ()\u003e`, factory\n  returns `OsrBuild { def, osr: Vec\u003cOsrEntry\u003e }`; publishes entry + table atomically.\n- `Bead::swap_compiled_with_osr(new_code, osr)`. Also `deopt.rs`.\n- Beadie's own docs suggest `site` = **MIR block id** — use that.\n\nrayzor side: `compiler/src/codegen/beadie_jit.rs:52` imports only\n`BackendAdapter, Bead, CompileError, CompileOutcome, JitBackend, ThresholdPolicy`.\ngrep for `on_invoke_osr|swap_compiled_with_osr|osr_entry|OsrBuild|OsrEntry` across\n`compiler/src` + `runtime/src` returns NOTHING. Only the call-count path was wired.\n\n**Why it matters — the two complaints are one defect.** Promotion swaps entries in\n`function_pointers` (tiered_backend.rs:310), so only a NEWLY ENTERED call gets the new\ntier; a frame already executing keeps its old machine code until it returns. A hot loop\nentered once therefore never reaches LLVM. `auto_upgrade_to_llvm_after_main_entry` +\n`compile_all_with_llvm()` (tiered_backend.rs:2112, :2840) compile the WHOLE module up\nfront purely to dodge that — that is the ~445ms CI compile overhead.\n\n**ORDERING TRAP: lazy per-function LLVM is NOT safe until OSR exists.** binarytrees would\nsurvive (bottomUpTree is called 29M times, promotes at a call boundary) but mandelbrot and\nnbody would fall off a cliff — their hot code is a loop inside a function entered once, so\nit would stay on Cranelift forever. OSR is the keystone; do it FIRST.\n\nWhat rayzor still has to build (beadie explicitly leaves it to the runtime —\n\"reconstructing the live state into that shape is the runtime's responsibility\"):\n1. OSR variant MIR functions: entry block = loop header, params = live-ins.\n2. Back-edge probes in generated code: bump counter, `osr_entry(site)`, transfer, return.\n\nPieces already in tree: `DominatorTree::compute`, `NaturalLoop { header,\nback_edge_source, blocks, exit_blocks, preheader }` + `LoopNestInfo::analyze`\n(ir/loop_analysis.rs), `IrPhiNode { dest, incoming, ty }` (ir/blocks.rs:78),\ncall-count profiling with thresholds (codegen/profiling.rs). Missing: any back-edge\ncounter — grep `back_edge|backedge` in codegen/ is empty.\n\nVariant construction (SSA makes it safe): region = blocks reachable from header;\nlive-ins = values used in region but defined outside; header phis become PARAMETERS\n(the back edge passes each phi's incoming value for `back_edge_source`); drop phi\nincomings from predecessors outside the region. Availability is guaranteed: if v is\nlive-in at the header then def(v) dominates the header, and the header dominates the\nlatch, so every live-in is available at the transfer point.\n\nSee [[bugs-restored-modules-lose-the-llvm-tier]].\n\n- Index summary moved from MEMORY.md (2026-10-18): Beadie SHIPS OSR; rayzor never wired it. STAGE 1 LANDED 81deab0d (variant builder, 9 tests, uncalled). Header phis MUST survive; live-ins are STRICT DOMINANCE not region membership. Lazy compile UNSAFE before OSR.\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `project_osr_beadie_helpers_unwired` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}